 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets deliver committed changes.** Each browser holds a server-owned socket session that streams committed revisions; the change store exposes replayable revisions for reconnect catch-up, and the browser owns subscription and reconnect. Considered five-second HTTPS polling (stateless and simplest to operate, but adds repeated reads and a few seconds of lag) and a hosted realtime service (less connection ops, but recurring fees and provider coupling). Self-hosting gives low-latency updates without a realtime vendor; the project takes on connection capacity, reliable publication, and reconnect handling as its own operational work. See [the decision doc](decisions/realtime-delivery-approach.md) for the full analysis.
